<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharae (Achaea)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharae was an ancient Greek city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, inland from Patrae, and one of the twelve cities of the original Achaean League. In 280 BCE it joined Dyme, Patrae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tritaea">Tritaea</a> in reviving the league, which grew into a major power in Hellenistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Pausanias later described its marketplace with a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> that gave oracles.</p></div>
